WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:04.320
Lots of recent free speech debates have come up in colleges. Here are 10 rules

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.680
for how the freedom of speech applies to
college students.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.420
1. Students of public colleges may not be disciplined for their speech,

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.260
unless it falls into the narrow First Amendment exceptions,

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:18.200
such as true threats of criminal attack or face-to-face personal insults

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.220
that are likely to start a fight. That's true even if the speech is seen as evil or offensive, whether

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.900
racist, sexist, religiously bigoted, unpatriotic supportive of crime or whatever else.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.920
For instance, a federal appeals court held that public university students can't be disciplined

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:37.340
for putting on an ugly woman skit at a fraternity event in which one of the students was in blackface.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:41.840
2. A public college can't limit broadly available benefits based on a groups viewpoint.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:47.280
It can't for instance, deny bulletin board space to groups that spread religious views, anti-homosexuality views,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.860
racially offensive parodies, or any other views.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:55.960
3. This is also true for student-run newspapers unless the college so controls the newspaper that it's viewed

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.520
as being partly the university's own speech.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:03.680
4. Inside the classroom though, the professor is in charge. Professors may orchestrate class discussions

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.180
in a way that they think brings out important ideas and facts and promotes student participation.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.320
That means they can cut off students who speak off topic or who insult their classmates.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.920
Professors can  also ask students to make the best argument for a particular viewpoint,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.020
and tell students that certain views, say that the earth is 6,000 years old, are wrong.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:24.660
We expect professors to be broad-minded on many issues and not unduly block student opinions

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.580
just because they disagree with those opinions.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.780
But the First Amendment doesn't give students a right to speak in the classroom

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.280
when the professor cuts them
off.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:37.100
5. Grading of student exams and papers likewise can't be content-neutral or even viewpoint-neutral.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:40.920
Professors shouldn't grade down students based on mere ideological disagreement

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.620
and colleges may forbid outright political discrimination by professors, but grading student work

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.580
inevitably requires a judgement about the quality of that work.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.240
And in many disciplines that will be a subjective judgment

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.140
based on more than just objectively determinable facts.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:02:00.780
6. Colleges and college departments can express their own views or can foster a selected set of views

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.020
without giving equal time to others.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:06.700
If a history department puts on a conference for instance, it can choose the panelists

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:11.400
based on their viewpoints even if that means excluding some viewpoints and preferring others.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.700
7.  People have no First Amendment right to shout down speakers,

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.700
whether the speaker is the guest speaker in a class, or speaker invited by a student group.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:23.800
Most speeches at colleges let listeners ask questions including critical ones during a Q&amp;A at the end,

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.140
and there's usually ample room to leaflet or protest on sidewalks outside the building.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:33.040
But it's perfectly constitutional to have content-neutral rules banning interruptions when a speaker has the floor,

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.220
and that give invited speakers the opportunity to speak without sharing that opportunities

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:38.360
with students or others.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:42.380
Indeed such rules may be necessary to make such speeches and debates possible.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:47.080
8. A public college has no First Amendment obligation to protect speakers against shouting down,

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.780
or even against violence. But it would violate the First Amendment for the college

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:55.900
to selectively refuse to protect speakers who express some views while protecting others.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:00.800
9. Content-neutral fees and restrictions are likely constitutional but a public college

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.340
probably violates student groups First Amendment rights when it charges high security fees

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.200
for invited speakers who express controversial views.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:12.840
10. All this of course applies only to public colleges, because the First Amendment only applies

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:16.320
to the federal, state and local governments, not to private organizations—

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.700
even private nonprofits that get tax exemptions and government subsidies.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:23.320
But many colleges voluntarily promise to uphold student free speech rights

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.120
as part of the college's commitment to academic freedom,

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.300
and as a way of attracting students and donors.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:32.680
And in California, a state statute applies some first amendment rules to private colleges

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.320
as well as public ones.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.320
So to sum up,

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.120
students at public colleges may not be disciplined for their speech,

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:42.000
a public college can't limit broadly available benefits based on the group's viewpoint.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.080
That's also true for student-run newspapers. Inside the classroom, the professor is in charge.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.400
Grading of student exams and papers can't be content-neutral or even viewpoint-neutral.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.740
Colleges and college departments can express their own views without giving equal time to others.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:57.660
People have no First Amendment right to shout down speakers,

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:02.160
a public college can't selectively protect some speakers but not others based on viewpoint,

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:05.340
content-neutral fees and restrictions are likely constitutional

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.860
and these rules generally only apply to public colleges,

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.860
but in California some of them apply to private colleges as well.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:14.240
This is not legal advice if this were legal advice it would be followed by a bill.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:15.300
Please use responsibly.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:19.300
Written by Eugene Bullock who's an actual real-life First Amendment law professor at UCLA.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.960
I'm Eugene Bullock and I approve this message.

